00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:02.440
Thank you so much, Christina.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:04.457
And I was just also
wondering listening

00:07:04.457 --> 00:07:06.040
to share about the
different packages,

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:12.550
how does the team decide what
to work on or prioritizing?

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:14.830
At the moment, the
priority has really

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:17.530
been developing a pipeline
for us that's usable.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:25.780
For us, the clock is
ticking for how long

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:27.850
we're going to keep using SAS.

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:29.658
Well, that's not true.

00:07:29.658 --> 00:07:30.700
We might always use that.

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:34.990
But we want a fully
developed pipeline for R.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:38.440
And so really,
like how I started

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:40.750
and how it's gone
from this point is

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:42.290
looking at this
pipeline and saying,

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:45.940
what do we need in order to
have the things that we need?

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:49.430
If we have all of these
things, we can report a study.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:53.170
And so really that was
where the target is for us.

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:58.300
And specifically,
for me and my team,

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:01.720
we try to identify where there's
not already a lot of people

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.680
doing the same thing.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:10.810
There's influx of people into
pharma building R packages

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:12.460
right now.

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:15.280
And there's a lot of
people doing things that

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:18.520
do calculations for instance.

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:21.670
And we're not necessarily--

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:25.000
I came to this role,
came into this world

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:26.710
by being a clinical
statistician.

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:32.409
I used to be the person who's
designed sample sized studies.

00:08:32.409 --> 00:08:35.740
And then I needed some things.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:37.960
And I was like, I'm going
to build a Shiny app to do

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:39.760
some sample size things for me.

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:43.030
And over time I became
that a Shiny person

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:44.830
who then built some packages.

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:46.120
And I then--

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.680
I more fell into this
role that was like,

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:50.980
I am, today, going to
be a data scientist.

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:53.170
It was really based on my
own needs and the needs

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:54.770
of people around me.

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:57.560
So that's how I fell in here.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.190
And so because I'm not
a clinical programmer,

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:03.910
I really do my best to
not take over some--

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:07.360
within the pharma space, some
of the reporting and stuff--

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:08.560
I guess, this is a very--

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:11.530
sorry, I'm very pharma
jargony right now.

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:15.040
Basically, within-- when you're
reporting a clinical trial,

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:20.410
there's two people who do the
database-- the data set things

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:22.210
in terms of job, career, people.

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:27.727
It's clinical programmers
and statisticians

00:09:27.727 --> 00:09:28.810
or clinical statisticians.

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:31.600
The statisticians
tend to maybe run

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:35.160
the final analysis, the
main big one that tells you

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:36.160
whether the study works.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:37.780
But there's a whole
bunch of things

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:40.870
that you need to do like,
get the number of people

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:42.355
in the study, check that--

00:09:42.355 --> 00:09:44.440
look at all the
safety, adverse events,

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:47.530
and calculate the number
of adverse events there

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:52.490
which are not really doing
traditional statistics.

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:54.770
They're more like means,
standard deviations,

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:58.145
all of the data sets cleaning
up, all of that sort of stuff.

00:09:58.145 --> 00:10:00.020
And that's traditionally
been done by someone

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:02.150
called a clinical programmer.

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:05.370
And so I have never been
one of those people.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:07.820
And so I always say I
just play one on TV.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:11.670
But I tried to leave the--

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:14.840
and my group isn't full of
people who were that role.

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:19.460
So I try to leave the stuff
where it's like you need really

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:23.150
in-depth knowledge to understand
how particular things are

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:25.010
calculated and data sets.

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:27.770
The people who work clinical
programmers are really good

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:30.560
at that because they can bring
that knowledge to the table,

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:31.340
because I can't.

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:34.850
That's why I tend to
avoid doing those things

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:38.990
and try to focus more on things
where I can add value that way,

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:40.540
so.

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:41.290
Thank you.

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:43.360
And I see there's
a lot of people

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:45.860
who have joined us since I first
said this in the beginning.

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:47.450
So I just want to
remind everybody--

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:49.492
I see a lot of questions
starting to come in now.

00:10:49.492 --> 00:10:51.550
But if you just join, welcome.

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:52.700
Thanks for joining us.

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:56.080
You can ask questions using
a Slido link if you want

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:57.610
to ask anything anonymously.

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:00.820
And we'll share that again
in the chat in a second.

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:03.830
But feel free to just raise
your hand to jump in as well.

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:07.272
I see Alan you just recently
put a question to the chat.

00:11:07.272 --> 00:11:08.230
Do you want to jump in?

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:14.287
Yeah, that was a good
coffee timing there.

00:11:14.287 --> 00:11:16.620
So Christina, I think I was
typing this at the same time

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:19.000
you were starting to describe
those other folks who

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.270
were downstream of
the work you're doing.

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:24.030
And I was just
interested in what

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:25.710
that collaboration is like.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:29.250
Are they-- if you think about
your most direct customers,

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:32.190
are they those clinical
programmers and others

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:35.070
who are on the study
specific teams,

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:38.020
or do you have a different--
what's the mode of interaction

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:38.520
there?

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:43.770
And how do you figure out
who to be responsive to

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:45.840
and when-- as
studies come along,

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:48.303
as study milestones are
hit, and things like that?

00:11:48.303 --> 00:11:50.970
Is that stuff you're engaged in,
or are you a little bit removed

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:56.170
from the study conduct itself?

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:59.360
I would say yes to everything.

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:02.470
So on one level, I'm
a little bit engaged--

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:06.040
removed from it and that I--

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:10.690
anything that I'm actively
building hopefully

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:13.690
isn't also being
used in a trial.

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:17.170
We try our best not
to be trying to fly

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:18.340
a machine while building it.

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:20.000
That seems not ideal.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.060
So we don't tend to do that.

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:25.720
But at the same time,
those clinical programmers

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:27.970
are oftentimes the
people who I work--

00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:29.200
I work with them a lot.

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:31.450
And they are major
customers for me

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:33.940
because if I write functions
that they don't understand

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:35.860
how they're going to
work together and fit

00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:37.300
and they don't
make sense to them

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:41.170
and don't solve problems
that they're having, they are

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:42.880
useless.

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:46.210
I might as well have drawn
a picture for myself.

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:48.820
That maybe was probably
would have been easier.

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:51.910
But they're also not my
only customers because--

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:53.350
depending on what I'm doing.

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:58.330
So for my data set,
metadata packages,

00:12:58.330 --> 00:13:00.730
they actually basically
are my only customers.

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:05.710
But for the table
formatting one,

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:07.748
there are other people
who are even further down

00:13:07.748 --> 00:13:09.790
the line than, for instance,
clinical programmer,

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:12.790
because a clinical programmer
makes all these tables to hand

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:16.960
over to somebody else called a
medical writer who then writes

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:20.640
the clinical study report
that goes to the FDA.

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:24.150
And so those people become--

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:28.430
are also my customers
because they have needs

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:33.793
like we need to ensure that
the typeface meets the FDA

00:13:33.793 --> 00:13:35.210
requirements,
because it turns out

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:38.360
the FDA only accepts
five different fonts

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:41.210
and that the margins have
to be very particular.

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:45.210
And there's all sorts of rules
that they know and I do not.

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:48.230
And so they are my
customers from that.

00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:50.960
And are the things
that I'm making still,

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:54.910
do they still meet those needs?

00:13:54.910 --> 00:13:55.480
Got it.

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:56.260
Cool.

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:57.250
Thank you.

00:13:57.250 --> 00:13:58.510
Yeah, of course.

00:13:58.510 --> 00:13:59.198
Hey, Marlene.

00:13:59.198 --> 00:14:00.740
I see you have your
hand raised, too.

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:02.540
Do you want to jump in?

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:03.500
Heck yeah, I do.

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:05.150
Good morning, everyone--
or I don't know what part

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:05.660
of the world you're in.

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:07.868
It may not be morning, but
it's morning where I'm at.

00:14:07.868 --> 00:14:11.540
Christina, thanks for joining
us and co-hosting with Rachael.

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:14.030
I am super thrilled
for you because I

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:16.470
feel like you're in a
place that I want to be at.

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:18.830
So I'm having a little
bit of envy here.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:22.220
But my question
stems from a twofold.

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:25.310
I'm trying to figure
out, structurally, where

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:27.000
you sit-in your organization.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:30.200
So are you under IT informatics?

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:32.340
Are you somewhere else?

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:36.870
Also what was the road like
to get to that position

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:39.920
and to have a pipeline
set up, because I'm

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:41.420
the only R user in my company.

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:43.010
And right now, I'm in--

00:14:43.010 --> 00:14:47.900
I'm scrambling to build kind of
a sandbox playground VM place

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:50.870
to start up some of
this work and then

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:55.220
to start-- to have to be the
pied piper for other analysts

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:59.005
and scientists in the company
to start to play with R

00:14:59.005 --> 00:15:02.198
and find a safe space
to work and then

00:15:02.198 --> 00:15:03.740
eventually, maybe
be in that position

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:05.660
where I write
packages like you do.

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:06.710
Yeah.

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:11.390
So my history-- so
I sit within what's

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:13.430
a department called
biostatistics.

00:15:13.430 --> 00:15:16.490
So in these massive
pharma companies,

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:19.130
you usually have a department
full of statisticians

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:22.820
and clinical programmers who do
the reporting out of the study

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:24.660
and the designing of the study.

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:26.870
So that's the department
that we sit-in.

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:31.790
And we grew out of a need
to support that department,

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:33.890
building out things like--

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:36.590
we actually started with really
supporting the statisticians

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:39.590
primarily and doing--
building tools for them

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:43.475
to help design trials.

00:15:43.475 --> 00:15:47.270
That was the need that I had
when I was a statistician.

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:50.450
And so that ended
up being the tool

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:55.580
that I built with
others, not just me.

00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:59.060
We have some really
brilliant statisticians here

00:15:59.060 --> 00:16:02.570
who know math more than I do.

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:04.580
And they're very
good at the math.

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:07.820
But maybe, they don't know
how to be-- how always to--

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:09.680
we are a big department.

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:12.020
GSK is a big company
that I don't even

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:13.430
know how many
statisticians there

00:16:13.430 --> 00:16:20.150
are but many, probably at
least 200 to 300 or something.

00:16:20.150 --> 00:16:23.810
The statistician who knows how
to do a particular analysis

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:28.220
type or like for trial design
can't sit with all 200 people

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:29.250
when they need help.

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:32.480
So we started building out
some Shiny apps to help them--

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:34.140
to help people design it.

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:35.900
So that is how we started.

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:40.848
We, being my entire
department, because I have--

00:16:40.848 --> 00:16:42.140
our department is not very old.

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:47.898
It's been around, God, four or
five-- four years, five years.

00:16:47.898 --> 00:16:49.440
So we're like a
fairly new department

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:53.400
within our sub department
with my larger biostatistics

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:54.990
department.

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:57.520
And I've been around
since the beginning.

00:16:57.520 --> 00:17:00.090
So that's how our
department largely started.

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:06.060
And then as we started building
those sorts of tools, some

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:08.250
of the-- there is a
request to, hey, we

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:12.329
want to move to trying R for
clinical study reporting.

00:17:12.329 --> 00:17:14.190
And so as we started
to move to that,

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:17.230
that's how we started building
packages for that need.

00:17:17.230 --> 00:17:20.338
And it was really--

00:17:20.338 --> 00:17:24.630
that's more or less how
it happened for both me

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:27.089
personally, and me, as a--

00:17:27.089 --> 00:17:30.025
we as a whole sub department.

00:17:33.140 --> 00:17:33.787
Thank you.

00:17:33.787 --> 00:17:34.370
MARLENE: Cool.

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:36.360
Thank you, yeah.

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:39.420
I want to make sure to get to
some of the anonymous questions

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:40.450
over on Slido, too.

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:42.190
So I'm going to go
jump back and forth.

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:45.330
But I see one over
there was, years ago,

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:50.320
GSK had a very specific
extensive SAS macro system.

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:53.010
It said, it even had a
name which I now forget.

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:55.470
Is the corp now
doing this with R?

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:03.540
So I don't know what
the grand vision is.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:07.350
I am not puppet master.

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:11.190
But I would say we
are really trying

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:15.270
to focus as much as possible as
doing stuff in the open source.

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:20.040
So I think a lot of the
extensive macro system

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:23.190
that we currently
have today, which

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:25.320
I don't know if I'm
allowed to say the name of,

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:26.700
I don't know what the rules are.

00:18:26.700 --> 00:18:28.200
I'm not going to
say the name of it,

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:32.660
because you didn't, so
just keep it that way.

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:36.900
It's like the one that
shall not be named.

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:41.070
That macro system--
a lot of what it does

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:45.840
are things that Admiral and
other pharmaverse packages are

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:47.790
taking into place and doing.

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:53.340
And really, our goal as
like the larger pharmaverse

00:18:53.340 --> 00:18:59.760
is to do our best not just at
GSK but other pharma companies

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:02.610
to as much as we can open
source all of these kind

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:07.420
of complex macro systems into
things that everybody can use.

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:08.970
It makes it easier for everyone.

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:11.200
And when we're hiring
people for instance,

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:13.230
we can hire someone and
then be like, yes, I

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:15.345
have used all of
these pharma specific,

00:19:15.345 --> 00:19:17.550
R packages because
I come from pharma.

00:19:17.550 --> 00:19:21.180
And we don't have to spend
six months or whatever

00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:24.150
training them for our
complex macro system.

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:27.240
Instead, it's more like, here
are some of our processes,

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:30.570
but you should feel comfortable
with most of these things

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:33.010
because you've probably been
somewhat exposed to them.

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:37.080
So that's one of
the reasons that GSK

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:40.710
is looking at
pharmaverse and helping

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:42.720
in [INAUDIBLE] that
involved in that way because

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:43.762
of those sorts of spaces.

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:46.790
Thank you.

00:19:46.790 --> 00:19:48.530
And just for
clarification because I

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:50.720
think this was another
earlier question

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:53.960
was what actually
is the pharmaverse.

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:54.780
Oh, so sorry.

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:55.280
I'm--

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:57.140
RACHAEL: No, no worries.

00:19:57.140 --> 00:20:00.020
This is when you go-- when you
spend too much time in pharma,

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:01.730
we all have so much jargon.

00:20:01.730 --> 00:20:03.860
And we say it to
each other so much

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:06.980
that we forget what
jargon even is.

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:07.550
And I am so--

00:20:07.550 --> 00:20:08.420
I think we all all do that.

00:20:08.420 --> 00:20:09.200
I do that here.

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:12.060
--so, sorry.

00:20:12.060 --> 00:20:14.630
So yes, the
pharmaverse is a group

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:17.660
of packages that
is all open source.

00:20:17.660 --> 00:20:19.610
Thank you, Eric.

00:20:19.610 --> 00:20:22.250
You just put, there's
a website just for it.

00:20:22.250 --> 00:20:24.410
But basically, it's
a series of packages

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:29.240
that really look on
building pharma end to end,

00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:32.360
because the pharma workflow
is somewhat standardized.

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:35.690
The FDA and the EMA
have requirements.

00:20:35.690 --> 00:20:37.610
They're like, you need
to have a data set

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:39.350
and it's called
an atom data set.

00:20:39.350 --> 00:20:42.150
And it looks exactly like this.

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:44.150
And you're like, OK, great.

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:48.380
And so there's not like
everyone does their own thing

00:20:48.380 --> 00:20:51.740
and gets to be creative about
how that data set works.

00:20:51.740 --> 00:20:53.630
If you do that, the
FDA will be like,

00:20:53.630 --> 00:20:57.120
no, thank you, go away, make
the thing I've told you to make.

00:20:57.120 --> 00:21:02.960
So because there's-- and
with standardization can come

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:08.690
automation and
overall improvements.

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:12.110
So that means that
we're really--

00:21:12.110 --> 00:21:14.720
and the pharmaverse reverse
is trying to get some of that

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:16.790
automation, and
that time saving,

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:22.571
and stuff like that
possible because you--

00:21:22.571 --> 00:21:23.310
because of that.

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:26.420
So that is what
the pharmaverse it.

00:21:26.420 --> 00:21:26.920
Cool.

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:27.520
Thank you.

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:30.130
We're going to make through
like five questions, Rachael.

00:21:30.130 --> 00:21:31.300
And I'm so sorry.

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:32.890
No, this is great.

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:35.680
And I love seeing everybody
helping out in the chat,

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:37.370
too, and sharing links as well.

00:21:37.370 --> 00:21:38.080
Thanks, Eric.

00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:40.640
I see, Eric, you had a
question earlier, too.

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:42.080
Do you want to jump in?

00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:45.550
Be glad to great to hear
from you again, Christina.

00:21:45.550 --> 00:21:49.690
I'm curious, back to your
discussion about obviously open

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:54.040
source work, how does your team
prioritize that type of work

00:21:54.040 --> 00:21:57.130
versus your more quote, unquote,
"day-to-day responsibilities"

00:21:57.130 --> 00:21:58.330
at GSK?

00:21:58.330 --> 00:22:01.060
And what is leadership
buy and then

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:03.220
to give your team
support to work on

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:05.452
say the pharmaverse
or other sets

00:22:05.452 --> 00:22:06.910
that are happening
in the industry,

00:22:06.910 --> 00:22:08.710
because I think it's
an exciting trend.

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:13.030
But admittedly, I do see
some pockets of industry

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:18.010
slow on the uptake on this being
a really core importance here.

00:22:18.010 --> 00:22:19.670
Just curious, your
thoughts on that.

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:20.170
Yeah.

00:22:20.170 --> 00:22:23.140
So thankfully, I work
under Andy Nichols

00:22:23.140 --> 00:22:25.690
who I don't know-- most people
probably won't know that name.

00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:29.470
But yeah, Eric knows that name
which is important someone

00:22:29.470 --> 00:22:30.460
does.

00:22:30.460 --> 00:22:33.230
He helps lead-- there's the
thing called the R validation

00:22:33.230 --> 00:22:33.730
hub.

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:38.600
He very much loves R
and the open source.

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:44.050
And so we had to do a
lot of groundwork laying.

00:22:44.050 --> 00:22:46.165
But now, most of
my time is actually

00:22:46.165 --> 00:22:48.790
spent on dealing stuff-- dealing
with stuff in the open source.

00:22:48.790 --> 00:22:53.200
I would say 80% of my time
is doing stuff like that

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:56.410
and building packages
for the open source

00:22:56.410 --> 00:23:00.550
and then sometimes building
internal like compendium

00:23:00.550 --> 00:23:03.370
packages to make those
open source packages

00:23:03.370 --> 00:23:05.110
work for us internally.

00:23:05.110 --> 00:23:12.280
But I would say, it's largely
open source work is what I do.

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:15.340
But that's not necessarily
how it started out.

00:23:15.340 --> 00:23:19.660
But I think having
leadership like having

00:23:19.660 --> 00:23:23.500
Andy do all of the talking to be
like, open source is important,

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:24.730
has been great.

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:30.370
So have yourself and Andy
is my top recommendation.

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:35.617
Outside of having yourself
an Andy, I think for us--

00:23:35.617 --> 00:23:37.200
the pharmaverse has
been really great,

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:38.970
because it has been
a collaboration where

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:42.060
other pharma companies have
also said it's important.

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:45.700
And so you're able to--

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:48.940
no one wants to be the only one
out there putting open store

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:50.370
stuff out.

00:23:50.370 --> 00:23:51.930
That feels very
uncomfortable when

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:54.472
you're like standing alone in
the wilderness and you're like,

00:23:54.472 --> 00:23:55.680
I am alone.

00:23:55.680 --> 00:23:58.930
But as everyone started
to put stuff together,

00:23:58.930 --> 00:24:02.130
now we can really
talk to our leadership

00:24:02.130 --> 00:24:04.650
like, it's important
to use open source,

00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:07.710
because if we don't
do it and if our voice

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:10.050
doesn't get heard
here, sometimes it

00:24:10.050 --> 00:24:12.060
means that the way we
want things to be done

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:15.270
might not be done that way,
because somebody else built it.

00:24:15.270 --> 00:24:17.400
And now, that's really popular.

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:19.500
And that other person
built it a while ago.

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:20.610
And everyone uses that.

00:24:20.610 --> 00:24:24.785
And it can be hard
to change course,

00:24:24.785 --> 00:24:26.160
change your ship
that's already--

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:29.790
I don't know some ship
analogy about moving ships.

00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:34.920
And so that is why I
think that's generally

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:38.190
the argument that we use when
talking about the open source.

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:41.375
And also, you'll
hear something--

00:24:41.375 --> 00:24:42.750
I can't remember
the exact phrase

00:24:42.750 --> 00:24:51.150
but pharma, my general career
path, we're not an IT company.

00:24:51.150 --> 00:24:53.640
The day GSK becomes
an IT company

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:57.280
is the day I'm like,
please, this is bad.

00:24:57.280 --> 00:24:59.340
So we don't want to
be an IT company.

00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:03.030
We want to deliver people,
make drugs, and help

00:25:03.030 --> 00:25:04.560
people, live better lives.

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:07.110
That's our goal as
a pharma company,

00:25:07.110 --> 00:25:08.610
not to become an IT company.

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:12.960
So us developing software
that helps everyone

00:25:12.960 --> 00:25:17.100
deliver on the thing that
we're all supposed to be doing.

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:19.050
It's easier to argue
that that's not

00:25:19.050 --> 00:25:20.640
intellectual property
for us because

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:24.750
our intellectual property tends
to be things like molecules.

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:30.165
And that's a thing that we
make, and that's what we do.

00:25:30.165 --> 00:25:32.620
We don't-- we're
not an IP company.

00:25:32.620 --> 00:25:37.440
And so that's the other
thing to just where we

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:41.670
are in in the world of stuff.

00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:47.710
I see a lot of excitement
around the pharmaverse

00:25:47.710 --> 00:25:50.680
and in the chat right now
Libby and John talking

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:53.510
about other industries
have something similar.

00:25:53.510 --> 00:25:54.850
John, do you want to jump in?

00:25:58.540 --> 00:25:59.620
Really, thank you.

00:25:59.620 --> 00:26:01.972
So Christina, Libby, and I,
and a lot of other people

00:26:01.972 --> 00:26:04.180
have been talking about
trying to do something that's

00:26:04.180 --> 00:26:07.990
inspired by the pharmaverse
but more for human resources

00:26:07.990 --> 00:26:10.450
and trying to pull together
people in different companies

00:26:10.450 --> 00:26:12.430
to build packages together.

00:26:12.430 --> 00:26:13.960
We have far less
standardization.

00:26:13.960 --> 00:26:16.270
And honestly, HR
analytics is pretty poor.

00:26:16.270 --> 00:26:19.000
So anything that can
help raise the bar

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:22.810
would be really good at 95%
of the places or probably 99%.

00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:25.210
So any suggestions
on how to kick

00:26:25.210 --> 00:26:28.450
start a movement of
getting people together

00:26:28.450 --> 00:26:31.420
or any lessons learned from
the pharmaverse about what

00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:34.880
we should do or avoid?

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:37.880
I don't personally
believe you need to have

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:40.790
a big splash in order to start.

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:44.930
I think if you wait for
a big beautiful website

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:48.410
and everything to be working
before you try and get

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:52.800
into anything, you're going to
just be waiting in a circle.

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:56.690
So I firmly believe
finding some friends

00:26:56.690 --> 00:27:01.890
and start developing packages
with them is a great choice.

00:27:01.890 --> 00:27:05.690
And it doesn't have
to be a huge number.

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:10.070
Honestly, I wasn't necessarily
run for the whole origin

00:27:10.070 --> 00:27:11.120
story of the pharmaverse.

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:14.420
But part of it grew
out of something

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:16.340
like Michael Ruemmler
and Michael--

00:27:16.340 --> 00:27:19.105
and Mike Stackhouse, two people
who are not here and probably

00:27:19.105 --> 00:27:19.730
you don't know.

00:27:19.730 --> 00:27:21.290
But basically,
they were like, we

00:27:21.290 --> 00:27:22.790
should build some
packages together.

00:27:22.790 --> 00:27:23.780
And I was like, OK.

00:27:23.780 --> 00:27:25.050
And they were like,
Christina, come help.

00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:26.030
And I was like, OK.

00:27:26.030 --> 00:27:30.110
And I built a package
for them, with Atorus,

00:27:30.110 --> 00:27:33.950
so a different company that is
not even a big pharma to CRO.

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:37.340
So we're like we and a CRO
built a package or two together.

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:40.730
And then the Mikes we're
having more conversations

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:41.480
with other people.

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:43.563
And all of a sudden, there
is a beautiful website.

00:27:43.563 --> 00:27:45.920
And I was like, wow,
everything's coming together.

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:51.200
And so to some extent, it's
just like talking to people

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:53.480
and then starting
to build stuff.

00:27:53.480 --> 00:27:55.160
Eventually, you'll
have enough stuff

00:27:55.160 --> 00:27:58.220
that you will have
your own verse.

00:27:58.220 --> 00:28:00.920
But I highly recommend starting.

00:28:00.920 --> 00:28:05.570
And in terms of the things
aren't standard, what do you do

00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:08.720
and how do you start,
sometimes a nice starting place

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:11.870
is a package that helps
standardize stuff.

00:28:11.870 --> 00:28:15.080
Metadata is not really in a
standard format in pharma.

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:19.160
Everyone has their own way
of holding this metadata.

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:22.280
It gets standardized to a
thing called a define XML,

00:28:22.280 --> 00:28:25.230
but that's only right
at the end of the day.

00:28:25.230 --> 00:28:28.400
Typically, that defined XML
comes together, as you're

00:28:28.400 --> 00:28:30.650
like sending the thing the FDA.

00:28:30.650 --> 00:28:32.950
It's not like that's--

00:28:32.950 --> 00:28:35.250
it's not ready at the beginning.

00:28:35.250 --> 00:28:37.488
And so one of the
first packages that I

00:28:37.488 --> 00:28:39.530
built as part of the
pharmaverse was this package

00:28:39.530 --> 00:28:41.330
called metacore.

00:28:41.330 --> 00:28:42.560
As I said, I'm like--

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:44.780
I hold many metadata
things, don't know why.

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:48.680
But part of the reason was
we wanted to build automation

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:50.240
off this metadata.

00:28:50.240 --> 00:28:53.690
But everyone held it in
a different Excel format,

00:28:53.690 --> 00:28:56.540
typically, or like
it was in a database.

00:28:56.540 --> 00:28:58.420
But it was their own way.

00:28:58.420 --> 00:29:00.320
They designed it their way.

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:01.950
It's different for everybody.

00:29:01.950 --> 00:29:05.600
So we needed to have a
thing that we could all

00:29:05.600 --> 00:29:07.790
look at and be like,
this is the thing,

00:29:07.790 --> 00:29:10.860
and we all use this thing,
and this is how it works.

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:15.380
So like what metacore came for
us is just like an R6 object.

00:29:15.380 --> 00:29:16.460
It's just an object.

00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:19.280
It's the most boring package
in the whole wide world.

00:29:19.280 --> 00:29:21.020
It does almost new things.

00:29:21.020 --> 00:29:23.030
It sits there and
you can poke at it.

00:29:23.030 --> 00:29:25.010
But like, that's it?

00:29:25.010 --> 00:29:28.922
But that enabled additional
automation to be run off.

00:29:28.922 --> 00:29:30.380
So then we have a
different package

00:29:30.380 --> 00:29:33.650
called metatools that does
a lot of this automation.

00:29:33.650 --> 00:29:35.930
But the first step
was getting that thing

00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:39.418
we could all agree on was
like the thing we were using.

00:29:39.418 --> 00:29:40.835
And it doesn't
have to be perfect.

00:29:44.435 --> 00:29:45.310
It's a great point.

00:29:45.310 --> 00:29:46.860
Thank you, Christina.

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:50.282
So we have a ton of questions
here on Slido as well

00:29:50.282 --> 00:29:51.490
that I see coming through it.

00:29:51.490 --> 00:29:54.570
So one anonymous one
was, the learning curve

00:29:54.570 --> 00:29:57.210
is steep from cozy
SAS programming

00:29:57.210 --> 00:30:00.870
to pharmaverse or
a read open source.

00:30:00.870 --> 00:30:04.290
Any suggestions for newbies
transitioning from SAS

00:30:04.290 --> 00:30:07.330
to R and Python?

00:30:07.330 --> 00:30:13.710
My first suggestion
is like try

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:16.350
doing a thing that
you generally know

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:17.760
how to do that thing intact.

00:30:17.760 --> 00:30:22.200
If you don't run AIML models
in SAS, don't be like,

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:25.190
I'm going to learn R and
start with AIML models

00:30:25.190 --> 00:30:26.940
because that's a thing
I've always wanted.

00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:28.260
I know it's a thing you--

00:30:28.260 --> 00:30:29.970
even if you want to
get there eventually,

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:32.012
it can be helpful to start
in a place where like,

00:30:32.012 --> 00:30:34.510
I know what this data set
should look like at the end.

00:30:34.510 --> 00:30:37.270
So I'm going to do
something similar.

00:30:37.270 --> 00:30:41.340
So that's my first
general recommendation.

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:45.180
But I would also say
like, I really like

00:30:45.180 --> 00:30:46.410
the R for Data Science book.

00:30:46.410 --> 00:30:48.510
I think that one's really good.

00:30:48.510 --> 00:30:52.890
We are trying to-- we being the
people of the pharmaverse are

00:30:52.890 --> 00:30:57.510
trying to pull out some
sample like sample code used

00:30:57.510 --> 00:31:02.088
to build a standard ADSL

00:31:02.088 --> 00:31:03.630
You're saying you're
coming from SAS.

00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:06.510
So I'm going to use
pharma words at you

00:31:06.510 --> 00:31:07.980
like a standard data set.

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:14.250
So you can start doing things
that you are familiar with.

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:17.460
But those are my
general suggestions.

00:31:17.460 --> 00:31:19.920
At some point, you
just got to do it.

00:31:19.920 --> 00:31:22.890
And that will make-- it
will get easier with time

00:31:22.890 --> 00:31:24.870
because it's December 1.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:28.080
I'll also recommend
like advent of code.

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:31.183
It's a thing I like to do.

00:31:31.183 --> 00:31:33.100
The questions get really
hard towards the end.

00:31:33.100 --> 00:31:35.350
And so if you don't-- can't
do them in R, don't worry,

00:31:35.350 --> 00:31:39.660
I can't do them in R either,
but just practicing problems.

00:31:42.330 --> 00:31:45.360
I think going from SAS can
be hard because it's not

00:31:45.360 --> 00:31:47.280
an object-oriented language.

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:49.920
Sorry, if you can
see my cat behind me.

00:31:49.920 --> 00:31:51.240
I'm sorry if you can hear him.

00:31:51.240 --> 00:31:52.500
He's decided now.

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:55.380
We love having cats
join the Hangouts, too.

00:31:55.380 --> 00:31:57.810
See if they want
to jump up the--

00:31:57.810 --> 00:32:00.120
He's calling for his people.

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:04.740
Anyway, but yeah, SAS
is not object-oriented.

00:32:04.740 --> 00:32:06.690
And I think getting
over that first hurdle

00:32:06.690 --> 00:32:09.710
of like having an
object-oriented language

00:32:09.710 --> 00:32:10.400
is the hardest.

00:32:10.400 --> 00:32:14.600
But then it does get
easier after that.

00:32:14.600 --> 00:32:16.392
And I see lots of
people posting links.

00:32:16.392 --> 00:32:17.600
Look at other people's links.

00:32:17.600 --> 00:32:20.825
They seem great.

00:32:20.825 --> 00:32:21.770
I really like that.

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:24.350
Don't start with AIML
models there, too.

00:32:24.350 --> 00:32:28.700
It's just reminding me,
Travis, of your LinkedIn post

00:32:28.700 --> 00:32:34.280
yesterday about Spotify wrapped
as the most popular data

00:32:34.280 --> 00:32:37.010
science right now, just
counting how many times you

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:38.870
listen to something.

00:32:38.870 --> 00:32:42.500
Travis, I see you asked
a question a bit earlier.

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:43.920
Do you want to jump in?

00:32:43.920 --> 00:32:44.420
Sure.

00:32:44.420 --> 00:32:46.130
Hey, Christina.

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:48.960
I have a question about
how you access data.

00:32:48.960 --> 00:32:51.050
So like one of these
boring admin questions

00:32:51.050 --> 00:32:55.160
but knowing how a lot of-- so
for those not in pharma, there

00:32:55.160 --> 00:32:58.520
are some software
giants in the space that

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:02.060
provide solutions to make
electronic case report

00:33:02.060 --> 00:33:03.420
forms possible.

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:06.110
So at the clinic or wherever
your clinical trial is being

00:33:06.110 --> 00:33:09.860
facilitated, people have to
enter into an interface like,

00:33:09.860 --> 00:33:12.350
this patient had this
disorder and this event

00:33:12.350 --> 00:33:13.653
happened on this day.

00:33:13.653 --> 00:33:16.070
So there's companies that are
called things like metadata,

00:33:16.070 --> 00:33:17.720
and viva, and inform.

00:33:17.720 --> 00:33:19.700
And the way they
deliver out data

00:33:19.700 --> 00:33:23.150
is typically not by way of
giving you access to an API

00:33:23.150 --> 00:33:24.530
or keys to a database.

00:33:24.530 --> 00:33:30.380
Rather, they have modules that
do nefarious things like email

00:33:30.380 --> 00:33:31.890
data to you.

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:35.780
Have you all
encountered a better

00:33:35.780 --> 00:33:37.760
way of making this
happen, because it's

00:33:37.760 --> 00:33:40.760
one part of the data stack
in pharma that feels woefully

00:33:40.760 --> 00:33:41.900
behind the times.

00:33:41.900 --> 00:33:44.660
Are you all hitting like
a research data warehouse

00:33:44.660 --> 00:33:48.080
or something more fancy
than just the nastiness

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:50.090
of unsecured data transfer?

00:33:50.090 --> 00:33:53.750
So short answer, no.

00:33:53.750 --> 00:33:56.450
Long answer, GSK has
a lovely department

00:33:56.450 --> 00:34:00.530
called Data Management
that manages that data

00:34:00.530 --> 00:34:04.680
and hands it to us in
biostatistics as SDTM data.

00:34:04.680 --> 00:34:09.020
So because we get SDTM data--

00:34:09.020 --> 00:34:10.969
and that's how our
departments work.

00:34:10.969 --> 00:34:14.150
And my job-- back
to priority, how

00:34:14.150 --> 00:34:17.840
does the priorities of what
packages get made for us today

00:34:17.840 --> 00:34:20.270
largely set on what
my department needs.

00:34:20.270 --> 00:34:23.719
My department doesn't have
to worry about yucky data

00:34:23.719 --> 00:34:26.630
formats to SDTM or
trying to get those

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:31.520
sorts of things from an
eCRF into an SDTM, which--

00:34:31.520 --> 00:34:34.290
so that is why I don't do that.

00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:36.650
And that's why we've
not looked at it yet.

00:34:36.650 --> 00:34:38.060
That's not a no ever.

00:34:38.060 --> 00:34:41.239
It's just a thankfully
it's not been my problem,

00:34:41.239 --> 00:34:43.080
but I sympathize.

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:44.940
Yeah, fair enough.

00:34:44.940 --> 00:34:45.440
Yeah.

00:34:45.440 --> 00:34:48.050
I'm interested because a
lot of these data managers

00:34:48.050 --> 00:34:50.420
are now being rebranded as
clinical data scientists,

00:34:50.420 --> 00:34:52.909
I think is the new phrase
that's it's going around.

00:34:52.909 --> 00:34:54.889
So is that happening
at GSK, too?

00:34:54.889 --> 00:34:58.430
Because you do more of
these semi-complicated data

00:34:58.430 --> 00:34:59.720
engineering things.

00:34:59.720 --> 00:35:01.820
I've heard the
name bandied around.

00:35:01.820 --> 00:35:03.320
I don't know if it's like a--

00:35:03.320 --> 00:35:07.028
going to be a GSK thing or
not or has been a GSK thing.

00:35:07.028 --> 00:35:09.320
Honestly, so many people are
changing their name titles

00:35:09.320 --> 00:35:10.790
to data scientists now.

00:35:10.790 --> 00:35:13.220
I don't always keep it straight.

00:35:13.220 --> 00:35:14.580
It's a cool club.

00:35:14.580 --> 00:35:15.080
Great.

00:35:15.080 --> 00:35:16.220
It's a great club.

00:35:16.220 --> 00:35:17.840
Everyone can be in the club.

00:35:17.840 --> 00:35:19.490
I'm not gatekeeping the club.

00:35:19.490 --> 00:35:22.710
I just don't remember who has
joined the club this week.

00:35:22.710 --> 00:35:27.890
So yeah, that's my question.

00:35:27.890 --> 00:35:30.770
Sorry, I don't have
more better answer.

00:35:30.770 --> 00:35:31.520
No, that's fine.

00:35:31.520 --> 00:35:33.190
Thank you.

