requirements that I was talking

00:38:32.700 --> 00:38:34.560
about before, where
they're like, it needs

00:38:34.560 --> 00:38:38.320
to have these particular
margins, this particular font,

00:38:38.320 --> 00:38:41.790
this is how we want it to look
to fit with our standards.

00:38:41.790 --> 00:38:43.920
And so that is--

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:45.780
that's kind of the
tables we tend--

00:38:45.780 --> 00:38:47.640
I tend to talk about.

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:52.320
And this year with my team,
we developed a package

00:38:52.320 --> 00:38:57.090
that takes something called an
analysis results data, which

00:38:57.090 --> 00:38:59.370
is a long skinny
data standard that

00:38:59.370 --> 00:39:02.370
see this is currently piloting.

00:39:02.370 --> 00:39:04.800
But basically,
the idea is rather

00:39:04.800 --> 00:39:08.160
than having everybody-- because
what happens for the most part

00:39:08.160 --> 00:39:10.620
today, I won't say
through all of pharma

00:39:10.620 --> 00:39:16.710
but through most of pharma,
is that a clinical programmer

00:39:16.710 --> 00:39:19.080
maybe their job today is
to make a demographics

00:39:19.080 --> 00:39:20.260
table for their study.

00:39:20.260 --> 00:39:22.302
So they're going to make
this demographics table.

00:39:22.302 --> 00:39:25.530
They're going to do all the
calculations that they need

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:29.520
in order to get the end that
the number of people who

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:36.060
are over 65, and the number
of people who are under 65,

00:39:36.060 --> 00:39:38.430
and all sorts of things,
the average weight,

00:39:38.430 --> 00:39:42.780
the percentage of people who are
over 65, whatever by treatment,

00:39:42.780 --> 00:39:43.860
they'll make that table.

00:39:43.860 --> 00:39:46.770
Then somebody else comes
in and QCs that table.

00:39:46.770 --> 00:39:50.290
But when they're QC-ing that
table, they might be like,

00:39:50.290 --> 00:39:52.560
oh, all the numbers look
good but the spaces are off.

00:39:52.560 --> 00:39:56.340
Now, I'm going to spend two
hours arguing with spaces, yay.

00:39:56.340 --> 00:39:59.310
And everyone has a bad
time, so we don't want that.

00:39:59.310 --> 00:40:00.420
That seems bad.

00:40:00.420 --> 00:40:04.170
So the analysis
results that idea

00:40:04.170 --> 00:40:07.140
is that rather than having
to QC a formatted thing,

00:40:07.140 --> 00:40:10.800
you can QC the analysis
results metadata which

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:11.730
is long and skinny.

00:40:11.730 --> 00:40:14.580
And the point is it's much
easier to QC, you can just

00:40:14.580 --> 00:40:17.310
put all your ends and
rows and percents and rows

00:40:17.310 --> 00:40:20.280
and make it tidy
data, everything

00:40:20.280 --> 00:40:21.450
is good for everybody,

00:40:21.450 --> 00:40:24.000
But then at the end of the
day, the medical writers still

00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:26.880
do want a thing that looks like
a table that's easy to read,

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:30.720
or the clinician who's
trying to understand,

00:40:30.720 --> 00:40:33.390
we've run this study,
the stats say this thing.

00:40:33.390 --> 00:40:37.770
But they need-- clinicians
look at things to understand

00:40:37.770 --> 00:40:39.490
what happened in the study.

00:40:39.490 --> 00:40:43.900
So we need to make
it so easy for them.

00:40:43.900 --> 00:40:48.540
So one of the things that we
did is create this package

00:40:48.540 --> 00:40:52.290
that extracts some of that
formatting into metadata so

00:40:52.290 --> 00:40:54.060
that the clinical
programmer can just

00:40:54.060 --> 00:40:56.760
create a long and skinny
data set, call their job done

00:40:56.760 --> 00:40:57.630
and dusted.

00:40:57.630 --> 00:41:00.390
And the metadata sits elsewhere.

00:41:00.390 --> 00:41:02.640
The metadata can be
created when you create

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:05.753
these mock tables which is--

00:41:05.753 --> 00:41:07.420
so sorry for anyone
who's not in pharma.

00:41:07.420 --> 00:41:08.740
This is such a pharma talk.

00:41:08.740 --> 00:41:10.960
But basically, before
you start the study,

00:41:10.960 --> 00:41:12.460
you draw out a picture.

00:41:12.460 --> 00:41:15.280
And you're like, I want
a demographics table

00:41:15.280 --> 00:41:16.690
that looks like this picture.

00:41:16.690 --> 00:41:18.700
And then-- so then the
clinical programmer

00:41:18.700 --> 00:41:20.860
has to do a thing that
looks like that picture.

00:41:20.860 --> 00:41:24.460
But our idea is rather
than have it as a picture,

00:41:24.460 --> 00:41:25.690
have it as metadata.

00:41:25.690 --> 00:41:27.250
And then the clinical
programmer just

00:41:27.250 --> 00:41:28.583
makes something long and skinny.

00:41:28.583 --> 00:41:32.050
And then because you wrote the
metadata to draw your picture,

00:41:32.050 --> 00:41:33.850
it will make your picture.

00:41:33.850 --> 00:41:37.790
And you will be happy because it
will be exactly what you said.

00:41:37.790 --> 00:41:43.960
And so that's how it
works if that makes any--

00:41:43.960 --> 00:41:46.750
trying to explain code
in abstract is hard.

00:41:46.750 --> 00:41:48.850
So that's our tabling package.

00:41:48.850 --> 00:41:50.740
It really is more
table and formatting.

00:41:50.740 --> 00:41:53.380
And yeah when, we
talk about tables,

00:41:53.380 --> 00:41:55.840
it's just like the
demographic table

00:41:55.840 --> 00:41:58.840
that the clinician will look
at or the medical writer.

00:42:02.910 --> 00:42:03.780
Thank you.

00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:07.410
And don't worry about the
pharma specific stuff.

00:42:07.410 --> 00:42:10.080
I see John, thank you,
said, knowing nothing

00:42:10.080 --> 00:42:13.270
