00:50:56.920 --> 00:51:03.000
So this is like one
of our newer R packages.

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:05.370
So it is something
that like, I maintain

00:51:05.370 --> 00:51:06.510
my team helped me build.

00:51:06.510 --> 00:51:08.580
I couldn't have built it
without Ellis and Becca,

00:51:08.580 --> 00:51:11.730
so just to give everyone
credit as the lead thing.

00:51:11.730 --> 00:51:15.810
But also part of what we built
is based off a very pharma

00:51:15.810 --> 00:51:17.230
specific workflow.

00:51:17.230 --> 00:51:19.170
So just like I'm going
to put it out there.

00:51:19.170 --> 00:51:25.170
And in pharma, we do a thing
where you make a table that is

00:51:25.170 --> 00:51:29.260
full of nothing but
X's, where you're like,

00:51:29.260 --> 00:51:31.770
this should be treatment,
this should be placebo,

00:51:31.770 --> 00:51:35.160
this is the label, and then
looks like I want this to go

00:51:35.160 --> 00:51:39.150
to xx.x because I
want it to go to one--

00:51:39.150 --> 00:51:42.105
I want it rounded
only once, or like--

00:51:42.105 --> 00:51:43.230
so you do this whole thing.

00:51:43.230 --> 00:51:46.170
You draw out oftentimes,
sometimes in Excel,

00:51:46.170 --> 00:51:49.540
sometimes in Word, the thing
you want it to look like.

00:51:49.540 --> 00:51:52.290
And that's what's
called your mock.

00:51:52.290 --> 00:51:55.470
So what we've done
is we've basically

00:51:55.470 --> 00:51:58.830
made it so that this T format
package is a package where

00:51:58.830 --> 00:52:02.160
you can put this metadata in
to say like, I want things

00:52:02.160 --> 00:52:06.300
that are labeled and for
instance, to be rounded

00:52:06.300 --> 00:52:07.590
to nothing.

00:52:07.590 --> 00:52:09.803
You just want no decimal places.

00:52:09.803 --> 00:52:11.220
But I want things
that are labeled

00:52:11.220 --> 00:52:14.340
as mean, that are part of,
I don't know, the group age

00:52:14.340 --> 00:52:15.930
to be rounded like this.

00:52:15.930 --> 00:52:17.930
And you put it all in.

00:52:17.930 --> 00:52:21.200
That then-- so that's
just an R script

00:52:21.200 --> 00:52:24.560
where you can put
all this metadata in.

00:52:24.560 --> 00:52:27.140
Our grand vision, although
we're not there yet,

00:52:27.140 --> 00:52:31.520
because time and the limited
number of people and resources,

00:52:31.520 --> 00:52:33.620
is to have that right
out to a JSON file.

00:52:33.620 --> 00:52:35.750
It kind of looks
like a JSON now.

00:52:35.750 --> 00:52:37.880
We just pretend in
our heart it is.

00:52:37.880 --> 00:52:41.870
When in reality, it's
just an R object.

00:52:41.870 --> 00:52:47.780
And that R object
is then what's used

00:52:47.780 --> 00:52:49.530
whenever you have the data.

00:52:49.530 --> 00:52:52.550
So the data has to get into
this semi-standard format called

00:52:52.550 --> 00:52:56.570
an analysis results data, which
is just tidy data honestly.

00:52:56.570 --> 00:52:58.520
You could use any
tidy data, data

00:52:58.520 --> 00:53:02.580
set as long as it's long
and skinny like that.

00:53:02.580 --> 00:53:07.950
And so the plan would be that
the clinical programmer just

00:53:07.950 --> 00:53:10.230
makes that data set.

00:53:10.230 --> 00:53:12.861
And then they can--

00:53:12.861 --> 00:53:18.180
we can store the metadata,
which is in our pretend JSON

00:53:18.180 --> 00:53:20.820
but is now just an R object.

00:53:20.820 --> 00:53:22.005
And you can just--

00:53:22.005 --> 00:53:23.760
it's basically kind
of plug and play.

00:53:23.760 --> 00:53:27.220
So you can put-- if you
have a really standard table

00:53:27.220 --> 00:53:30.360
for instance, you can use the
same metadata over, and over,

00:53:30.360 --> 00:53:32.538
and over again and just
swap out the data sets.

00:53:32.538 --> 00:53:34.080
You can go as many
times as you like.

00:53:34.080 --> 00:53:36.840
And we'll make-- at the
moment, it goes out to DT,

00:53:36.840 --> 00:53:39.600
probably keep that forever
because DT is great.

00:53:39.600 --> 00:53:42.000
Like Rich has done
a really good job.

00:53:42.000 --> 00:53:43.980
It makes all the
things that we want it

00:53:43.980 --> 00:53:46.320
to look like for the most part.

00:53:46.320 --> 00:53:48.810
And we've had to work
with Rich a little bit

00:53:48.810 --> 00:53:50.440
in order to get things--

00:53:50.440 --> 00:53:53.100
so there are some additional
things that we've needed.

00:53:53.100 --> 00:53:55.410
And Ellis worked
with Rich in order

00:53:55.410 --> 00:53:57.405
to get it to write out
to Word for instance,

00:53:57.405 --> 00:53:58.530
which was important for us.

00:53:58.530 --> 00:54:01.300
And he did that and
that was amazing.

00:54:01.300 --> 00:54:09.156
And so it's-- we work
with people like Rich to--

00:54:09.156 --> 00:54:12.560
our goal as a GSK
department largely

00:54:12.560 --> 00:54:14.810
is to not build and
maintain everything

00:54:14.810 --> 00:54:17.580
but to work with great fit
tools that already exist.

00:54:17.580 --> 00:54:19.950
So yeah, that was--

00:54:19.950 --> 00:54:21.753
So that's how we
would see it work

00:54:21.753 --> 00:54:23.670
is that once you have
this [INAUDIBLE] object,

00:54:23.670 --> 00:54:25.337
it can go to all of
the things you want.

00:54:25.337 --> 00:54:28.490
It can go to your PowerPoint
or it can you go to PDF.

00:54:28.490 --> 00:54:29.360
It can go to Word.

00:54:29.360 --> 00:54:34.940
It can go to RTF,
HTML, whatever.

00:54:34.940 --> 00:54:39.680
And from our perspective, we
can hold the formatting metadata

00:54:39.680 --> 00:54:42.980
in a pretend JSON and
then just apply it

00:54:42.980 --> 00:54:46.130
to as many data sets as you
want it to be applied to.

00:54:49.183 --> 00:54:50.350
That's really fascinating.

00:54:50.350 --> 00:54:53.710
I will put in the chat
the thing-- actually,

00:54:53.710 --> 00:54:54.790
I can do that.

00:54:54.790 --> 00:54:58.120
I don't have the site off
the top of my head right

00:54:58.120 --> 00:54:59.480
this second.

00:54:59.480 --> 00:55:00.820
Eric, you've been really good.

00:55:00.820 --> 00:55:02.530
Can you pull up the
T format website?

00:55:02.530 --> 00:55:04.060
Do you know where--
do you have it?

00:55:04.060 --> 00:55:07.260
I'm just asking you because
you might note that's--

00:55:07.260 --> 00:55:09.260
I will get it shortly.

00:55:09.260 --> 00:55:11.760
It's on the way.

00:55:11.760 --> 00:55:13.370
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:55:13.370 --> 00:55:13.870
Yeah.

00:55:13.870 --> 00:55:18.070
So Tplyr does something similar,
except it does the analysis

00:55:18.070 --> 00:55:19.580
as well.

00:55:19.580 --> 00:55:22.750
And our goal is to not
do-- to separate out

00:55:22.750 --> 00:55:24.490
the analysis and the formatting.

00:55:24.490 --> 00:55:28.000
Because oftentimes, you want to
change your mind on formatting.

00:55:28.000 --> 00:55:29.920
There are times that you--

00:55:29.920 --> 00:55:31.630
especially in pharma,
there are times

00:55:31.630 --> 00:55:35.000
where you're presenting some
slides to someone or something.

00:55:35.000 --> 00:55:39.670
And so you don't need it rounded
out to the exact right decimal.

00:55:39.670 --> 00:55:41.920
Or because you're just
trying to tell a story.

00:55:41.920 --> 00:55:45.477
Or, for instance, once you
finish the clinical study

00:55:45.477 --> 00:55:47.560
report, send it to the
FDA, and all of the things,

00:55:47.560 --> 00:55:50.530
you do need to publish it.

00:55:50.530 --> 00:55:51.760
We publish these papers.

00:55:51.760 --> 00:55:55.300
And so sometimes, journals are
like, we have p-value rules,

00:55:55.300 --> 00:55:57.550
and your p-value needs to
be rounded differently.

00:55:57.550 --> 00:56:01.707
And if you only save
the rounded p-value,

00:56:01.707 --> 00:56:03.040
you can't round a rounded value.

00:56:03.040 --> 00:56:03.540
Right.

00:56:03.540 --> 00:56:06.980
So you might have to rerun
the analysis, which is bad.

00:56:06.980 --> 00:56:09.560
We don't want to do that because
you have re-QC everything.

00:56:09.560 --> 00:56:14.050
So our goal is to
separate out the analysis

00:56:14.050 --> 00:56:15.220
and the formatting.

00:56:15.220 --> 00:56:18.423
And so that's what kind
of T Format does do.

00:56:18.423 --> 00:56:19.090
Thank you, Eric.

00:56:19.090 --> 00:56:21.670
You're the best.

00:56:21.670 --> 00:56:22.420
Thank you.

